  "title": "Peace, Pleasing and War: pac, plac and bell",
  "short": "pac means peace, plac means please or calm and bell means war.",
  "trouble": "bell (war) is not the bell you ring or belle (a beauty): belligerent means warlike.",
  "prereqs": [
    "roots.cred-fid-ver"
  ],
  "xp": 1,
  "viz": [
    "vo-roots",
    "vo-word"
  ],
  "figureArchetype": "GeneralFigure",
  "practice": null,
  "lesson": {
    "blocks": [
      {
        "t": "p",
        "html": "The largest ocean on Earth is the Pacific, whose name means “peaceful.” <b>pac</b> (also <b>pax</b>) means “peace.” A close neighbor, <b>plac</b>, means “please” or “calm”: a <i>placid</i> lake is calm. And the opposite of peace, <b>bell</b>, means “war”: a <i>rebel</i> wages war again."
      },
      {
        "t": "viz",
        "name": "vo-scene",
        "opts": {
          "scene": {
            "bg": "home",
            "cast": [
              {
                "who": "lou",
                "x": 36,
                "pose": "give",
                "holds": "teddy",
                "hsize": 1.4,
                "mood": "calm",
                "face": "right"
              },
              {
                "who": "theo",
                "x": 66,
                "mood": "happy",
                "face": "left"
              }
            ],
            "fx": [
              {
                "kind": "hearts",
                "on": "c1",
                "at": "head"
              }
            ],
            "labels": [
              {
                "text": "PAC-IFY: make peace",
                "x": 50,
                "y": 92
              }
            ],
            "alt": "A grandmother hands a teddy bear to a toddler, who stops crying and smiles."
          }
        },
        "cap": "A grandmother hands a teddy bear to a toddler, who stops crying and smiles."
      },
      {
        "t": "key",
        "html": "<b>pac</b>, <b>pax</b> = <b>peace</b>. <b>plac</b> = <b>please, calm</b>. <b>bell</b> = <b>war</b>."
      },
      {
        "t": "p",
        "html": "The <i>pac</i> and <i>plac</i> words are about calming people down: to <b>pacify</b> is to make peaceful, to <b>placate</b> is to please someone angry, and to <b>appease</b> is to bring to peace (it comes through French <i>pais</i>, from <i>pax</i>). The <i>bell</i> words describe the opposite mood: <b>belligerent</b> (waging war, hostile) and <b>bellicose</b> (eager to fight)."
      },
      {
        "t": "h",
        "text": "The parts and their words"
      },
      {
        "t": "viz",
        "name": "vo-roots",
        "opts": {
          "max": 10,
          "roots": [
            "pac",
            "plac",
            "bell"
          ]
        },
        "cap": "Words on the list built on pac, plac and bell."
      },
      {
        "t": "h",
        "text": "Words taken apart"
      },
      {
        "t": "table",
        "head": [
          "Word and meaning",
          "Parts, and what they say"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            "<b>pacify</b><br>to calm or bring peace to",
            "<b>pac</b> <i>peace</i> + -ify <i>to make</i><br>→ “to make peaceful”"
          ],
          [
            "<b>appease</b><br>to calm someone by giving them what they want",
            "ap- <i>to</i> + <b>pease</b> <i>peace</i><br>→ “bring to peace”"
          ],
          [
            "<b>placate</b><br>to calm someone’s anger by giving in a little",
            "<b>plac</b> <i>please</i> + -ate<br>→ “to please (someone angry)”"
          ],
          [
            "<b>placid</b><br>calm and peaceful",
            "<b>plac</b> <i>calm</i> + -id<br>→ “calm, pleasing”"
          ],
          [
            "<b>implacable</b><br>impossible to calm or satisfy",
            "im- <i>not</i> + <b>plac</b> <i>please</i> + -able <i>able to be</i><br>→ “not able to be pleased (or calmed)”"
          ],
          [
            "<b>complacent</b><br>too pleased with oneself to see dangers or the need to improve",
            "com- <i>thoroughly</i> + <b>plac</b> <i>please</i> + -ent<br>→ “thoroughly pleased (with oneself)”"
          ],
          [
            "<b>belligerent</b><br>hostile and aggressive",
            "<b>bell</b> <i>war</i> + ger <i>wage</i> + -ent<br>→ “waging war”"
          ],
          [
            "<b>bellicose</b><br>eager to fight",
            "<b>bell</b> <i>war</i> + -icose <i>full of</i><br>→ “full of war”"
          ]
        ],
        "cap": "Each word, what it means now, and its parts."
      },
      {
        "t": "ex",
        "title": "Use the parts",
        "q": "Nothing calmed the angry customer: not an apology, not a refund. He was <b>implacable</b>. What does the word mean?",
        "steps": [
          "Take it apart: <b>im-</b> (not) + <b>plac</b> (please, calm) + <b>-able</b> (able to be).",
          "Literally: “not able to be calmed.”",
          "The sentence fits: nothing calmed him."
        ],
        "a": "Impossible to calm or satisfy."
      },
